 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335336 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335336 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186333376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335336 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "31501bbf-eed4-56b3-a098-ce6e49b75221" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335418 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335418 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335418 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335418 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335418 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334478 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335418 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335418 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335418 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186333202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335418 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5bd33408-f473-5e92-b602-88c8f1b4211b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335438 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335438 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335438 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335438 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334145 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335438 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335438 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335438 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335438 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186334363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335438 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "66899f0d-563e-5ed9-a6cb-77e4a5b3f25b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186333415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186334908 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186335212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335669 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335669 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335669 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186333072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335669 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f5f3f896-e079-54b5-988f-9af076e4c242" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335843 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335282 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335843 #edb/ref 1069 "q-65508" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335843 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335843 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335843 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "96ac7103-36fb-556e-9958-61a600af1557" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335843 #edb/ref 1074 "The difference formula for cosine gives  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=1 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =1 \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={1}^{2}+{1}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =2 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =2 \\\\ R & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335893 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335438 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335893 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66669" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335893 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335893 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=(x-2{)}^{2},$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (2,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-1,5)$\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,2]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335893 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "26ae354c-e4a4-50d9-b820-8ba146650b10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335893 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph of the function $f(x)=(x-2{)}^{2},$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units  to  the right  $f(x)=(x-2{)}^{2}.$\n\n![](images/1e/1e3ac550e35a3dc93b3e26e5834f763847b04f5133de02e368124f54dd2b9dd6.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-1,5).$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/3e/3ebb316ca122b6786b8ef501fc7c56f8e0e68c8cfc32e5801c493d38382fc26c.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the intervals $x\\in (2,\\infty )$  and $x\\in (-\\infty ,2].$  On these intervals, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/86/86c7c9cacc7a3868565f33130563ffed0ca423aa087e76cc08d103ea171822ed.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335899 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335899 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66679" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335899 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335899 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}-2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$\n- $x\\in (1,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-1,3]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335899 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ffb9d3e2-6b9a-5fb3-b64e-0b7d921759d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335899 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph of the function $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}-2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\n- Translate it by $1$ units to the right,  to get $y=(x-1)^{2}.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=(x-1)^{2}$  by $2$ units down to get $f(x)=(x-1)^{2}-2.$\n\n![](images/f5/f53a4f97166877085c90acc01e3f8d0c6ac7bb3f704bbfb792d747122d314013.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$ and $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$ On these intervals, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/b0/b088c8ed1974a7c259dd54f85aec7a124579981971babfe8dee0313ca23b6458.png)\n\n  \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-1,3].$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/b3/b3af13782a01213bb09fdeef38fbeb3fbca3f41348036e6786b4efa74a0166b4.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335904 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335904 #edb/ref 1069 "q-66729" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335904 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335904 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(x)=|x-1|-2,$ on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$\n- $x\\in (1,\\infty )$\n- $x\\in (-\\infty ,2]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335904 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0ee60b09-63e9-5076-abae-7337fc233de5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335904 #edb/ref 1074 "The graph of the function $f(x)=|x-1|-2$ can be obtained in the following way:\n\n- Take the curve $y=|x|.$\n\n- Translate it by $1$ units to the right,  to get $y=|x-1|.$\n\n- Finally, translate $y=|x-1|$ by $2$ units down to get $f(x)=|x-1|-2.$\n\n![](images/75/753840da17642582f9dbc0debc18f1fa3efcbbb11142894c1dd823ad6c0c630a.png)\n\nA function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test. \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-\\infty ,1]$ and $x\\in (1,\\infty ).$ On these intervals, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/15/15f1c797cbddd2ac8e2e9c5be72f842e7d1accfc5f619af57b1f35a27e1015c0.png)\n\n  \n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on the interval $x\\in (-\\infty ,2].$ On this interval, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/a1/a100489b49da7b06c98783b1b1c4ec0de9d891fc2b22f4b36b1a0ac2d54fc4f3.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I  and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186335336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335994 #edb/ref 1069 "q-69718" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335994 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335994 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Pascal's triangle to determine the value of $(\\frac{4}{2}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335994 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a02a9e84-58b4-5bd5-9076-a8eafa7a835e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186335994 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the value of $(\\frac{4}{2}),$ we need to find the number that is located in row $4$ and position $2$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important**: When counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero! Remember:\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe value located in row $4$ and position $2$ is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(\\frac{4}{2})=6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336121 #edb/ref 1080 "12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336121 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336121 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09ea4053-49e8-5168-b098-82430469625d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336161 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336161 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336161 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ddc44f3-1a32-52a9-9031-b6a56a4c5108" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336170 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336170 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336170 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ea49bc6-7ef7-57db-bb92-09c032a1d6f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336232 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref