#edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338800 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338800 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70667" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338800 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338800 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements regarding the graph of $y=cos⁡x$ are true?\n\n- The graph intersects the $y$-axis at $y=1$\n- The function has a minimum value at $x=-\\pi .$\n- The range of the function is $[-\\pi ,\\pi ].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338800 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "24b90149-221d-555e-91aa-b1d1cb7cb1c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338800 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The $y$-intercepts of the function is $y=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function at $x=-\\pi $ has a minimum value.\n\n- Statement III is false. The minimum value is $-1$, the maximum value is $1$, and so the range is $[-1,1].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338801 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338801 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70669" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338801 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338801 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=cot⁡x?$ Assume $n$ is an integer.\n\n- The function has no minimum value. $$\n- $x=-4\\pi $ is in the domain of the function. $$\n- $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a zero of the function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338801 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a752d35-73b8-55ea-bb50-4d11f60be65b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338801 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's plot the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](images/8b/8bc549c68a3bc5b7989ac1835e4bb58183066c7797264b8cccd4f3fd2c895056.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has no minimum or maximum value. $$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is defined for all real numbers except the values $n\\pi $ for any integer $n$, thus it is not defined for $x=-4\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is true. The zeros of $y=cot⁡x$ occur at $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ for any integer $n.$ Therefore, $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a zero of the function." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338891 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338891 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338891 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338891 #edb/ref 1071 "For what reason does the function $f(x)$ given below have a discontinuity at $x=-1?$\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2}, & x<-1 \\\\ 2, & x=-1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}, & x>-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338891 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c90a0801-6b26-5a6a-b72a-693261024597" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338891 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's check the conditions necessary for $f(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-1.$\n\n- $f(-1)=2,$ so $f(-1)$ exists. $✓$\n\n- We calculate the one-sided limits and check if they coincide:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}(\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}+1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+1}{2} \\\\  & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1$ $✓$\n\n- The limit of the function is not equal to the function value: ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)=1,$ while $f(-1)=2.$ $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-1$ because ${lim}_{x\\to (-1)}f(x)\\ne f(-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338981 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186338472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338981 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71734" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338981 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338981 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the augmented matrix for the following system of linear equations?\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}u-v=-1 \\\\ -u-v=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338981 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1731054d-ea13-5a85-8abe-142bb7f3ad7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186338981 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the system is already organized so that the variables are in the order $u,v$ on the left-hand side, the constants are on the right-hand side, and every variable has a coefficient.   \n\nThe coefficient matrix consists of the coefficients of the variables: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe augmented matrix is the coefficient matrix augmented with the column that represents the right-hand sides of the equations:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339164 #edb/ref 1080 "-11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339164 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339164 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "04ad9e02-0e28-5883-8383-80771f092ba1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339175 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}6+3y-5z \\\\ -4-2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339175 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339175 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "05a17e67-d12e-5f24-bd45-6190f67c0403" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339197 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339197 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339197 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "085a372e-3d44-5218-8ffc-7245e73f20e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339203 #edb/ref 1080 "⟨11,-8,0⟩" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339203 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186339203 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "08c85a87-f9d3-5992-bd65-769c46e1d